    Y --> E([End: Initiation considered])
    N --> E
    %%action startClientOnboarding trigger-workflow when: status == "started" workflow: ClientOnboarding message: Start the recorded onboarding process

%% ---- The reporting pass (llmdetailed §10.5.1) -----------------------------
%%
%% What the derived pack already gives this model without being asked: a
%% register per entity, a breakdown per %%enum-bound column, created-per-month,
%% a lifecycle report over every state machine in the diagram's own order, and
%% children-per-parent for every relationship. None of that needed anyone who
%% has advised a client.
%%
%% These do. Each of the six roles on the %%rbac matrix above is walked through
%% the four questions — what they open first, what tells them something is wrong
%% today, what they are asked for weekly, and what rots silently if nobody
%% looks. Every query reads only bus_ tables, because that is all a reporting
%% role is permitted to read, and every one has been run against the schema this
%% model generates. The test is the role's own job, not a count.

%% -- advisor: the book they are responsible for --------------------------------
    %%report advisor-clients-stuck-onboarding title: Clients who never finished onboarding entity: Client help: The first question of the week. A client in onboarding or pending KYC past a month is revenue that has been sold and not started, and every one of them is waiting on a document somebody forgot to chase. sql: SELECT c.client_number, y.display_name AS client, c.status, c.onboarding_date, EXTRACT(day FROM now() - c.onboarding_date)::int AS days_since FROM bus_client c LEFT JOIN bus_party y ON y.id = c.party_id WHERE c.deleted_at IS NULL AND c.status IN ('onboarding', 'pending_kyc') AND c.onboarding_date < now()::date - 30 ORDER BY days_since DESC
    %%report advisor-clients-without-portfolio title: Active clients with no portfolio entity: Client help: An active client with nothing invested is either a sale that stopped halfway or a data error, and both look identical from the client screen. Only the absence of a portfolio shows it. sql: SELECT c.client_number, y.display_name AS client, c.client_type, c.service_model, c.onboarding_date FROM bus_client c LEFT JOIN bus_party y ON y.id = c.party_id LEFT JOIN bus_portfolio p ON p.household_id = c.household_id AND p.deleted_at IS NULL AND p.status = 'active' WHERE c.deleted_at IS NULL AND c.status = 'active' GROUP BY c.id, c.client_number, y.display_name, c.client_type, c.service_model, c.onboarding_date HAVING COUNT(p.id) = 0 ORDER BY c.onboarding_date
    %%report advisor-book-by-service-model title: The book, by service model entity: Client chart: bar x: service_model y: clients help: What an advisor is actually carrying, which is the conversation about capacity. A book that is mostly the highest-touch model is one that cannot take another client, whatever the headcount says. sql: SELECT COALESCE(NULLIF(c.service_model, ''), '(not set)') AS service_model, COUNT(*) AS clients, COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE c.status = 'active') AS active FROM bus_client c WHERE c.deleted_at IS NULL AND c.status NOT IN ('closed', 'inactive') GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY clients DESC
    %%report advisor-dormant-clients title: Active clients nobody has spoken to entity: Client help: The account that rots silently. An active client with no interaction recorded in six months has usually already been approached by somebody else, and the first anyone here hears of it is the transfer request. sql: SELECT c.client_number, y.display_name AS client, c.status, MAX(i.interaction_at) AS last_contact FROM bus_client c LEFT JOIN bus_party y ON y.id = c.party_id LEFT JOIN bus_interaction i ON i.party_id = c.party_id AND i.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE c.deleted_at IS NULL AND c.status = 'active' GROUP BY c.id, c.client_number, y.display_name, c.status HAVING MAX(i.interaction_at) IS NULL OR MAX(i.interaction_at) < now() - interval '180 days' ORDER BY last_contact NULLS FIRST
    %%report advisor-reviews-overdue title: Client reviews that are due and not held entity: ClientReview help: The review cycle is a promise to the client, and a review that stays due is a promise being broken quietly. Oldest first, because the longest wait is the likeliest complaint. sql: SELECT c.client_number, y.display_name AS client, r.status, r.review_date, r.next_review_date, (current_date - r.review_date) AS days_waiting FROM bus_client_review r JOIN bus_client c ON c.id = r.client_id LEFT JOIN bus_party y ON y.id = c.party_id WHERE r.deleted_at IS NULL AND r.status IN ('due', 'follow_up') AND r.review_date < current_date ORDER BY r.review_date
    %%report advisor-activities-overdue title: Follow-ups past their due date entity: Activity help: The task list as an advisor actually needs it: not everything planned, only what was promised for a day that has gone. Each row is a call or a document somebody is waiting on. sql: SELECT c.client_number, y.display_name AS client, a.activity_type, a.subject, a.priority, a.due_date, (current_date - a.due_date) AS days_overdue FROM bus_activity a LEFT JOIN bus_client c ON c.id = a.client_id LEFT JOIN bus_party y ON y.id = c.party_id WHERE a.deleted_at IS NULL AND a.status IN ('planned', 'in_progress') AND a.due_date < current_date ORDER BY a.due_date
    %%report advisor-goals-at-risk title: Goals that are close and underfunded entity: Goal help: A goal with a date inside three years and under half its money is the planning conversation that cannot wait for the annual review. Sorted by how little is funded, because that is the size of the gap the client has not been told about. sql: SELECT g.name AS goal, g.goal_type, g.target_amount, g.target_date, g.funded_percentage, g.priority FROM bus_goal g WHERE g.deleted_at IS NULL AND g.status IN ('identified', 'planned') AND g.target_date <= current_date + interval '3 years' AND COALESCE(g.funded_percentage, 0) < 50 ORDER BY COALESCE(g.funded_percentage, 0), g.target_date
    %%report advisor-recommendations-awaiting-approval title: Recommendations waiting for sign-off entity: InvestmentRecommendation help: Advice that has been written and not released. The client is waiting on a decision that is internal, so every day here is a day the market moves under a recommendation nobody has yet approved. sql: SELECT r.recommendation_date, r.recommendation_type, r.status, r.suitability_status, (current_date - r.recommendation_date) AS days_waiting FROM bus_investment_recommendation r WHERE r.deleted_at IS NULL AND r.status = 'pending_approval' ORDER BY r.recommendation_date
    %%report advisor-service-requests-open title: Open service requests by type entity: ServiceRequest chart: bar x: request_type y: requests help: What clients are asking for and have not received. The shape of this list is the shape of the service model: a single request type growing is a process that should be a product. sql: SELECT s.request_type, COUNT(*) AS requests, COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE s.priority = 'high') AS high_priority FROM bus_service_request s WHERE s.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.status NOT IN ('completed', 'rejected') GROUP BY s.request_type ORDER BY requests DESC
    %%report advisor-portfolios-behind-benchmark title: Portfolios behind their benchmark entity: PerformanceSnapshot help: The latest snapshot of each portfolio, kept only where the portfolio returned less than its benchmark. A shortfall is not a failure by itself, but it is the first thing a client reads in a statement and the last thing an advisor should hear about from them. sql: SELECT p.portfolio_name, s.snapshot_date, s.time_weighted_return, s.benchmark_return, (s.time_weighted_return - s.benchmark_return) AS difference FROM (SELECT DISTINCT ON (portfolio_id) * FROM bus_performance_snapshot WHERE deleted_at IS NULL ORDER BY portfolio_id, snapshot_date DESC) s JOIN bus_portfolio p ON p.id = s.portfolio_id WHERE p.deleted_at IS NULL AND p.status = 'active' AND s.time_weighted_return < s.benchmark_return ORDER BY difference
    %%report advisor-onboarding-pipeline title: Onboarding cases by stage entity: OnboardingCase chart: bar x: stage y: cases help: Where new clients are standing between the first meeting and the first investment. A stage that holds many cases and few leavers is the bottleneck, and it is rarely the stage people expect. sql: SELECT o.status AS stage, COUNT(*) AS cases, MIN(o.started_date) AS oldest_started FROM bus_onboarding_case o WHERE o.deleted_at IS NULL AND o.status NOT IN ('active', 'rejected', 'cancelled') GROUP BY o.status ORDER BY cases DESC

%% -- client: what the client themself is waiting on ----------------------------
    %%report client-proposals-awaiting-answer title: Proposals presented and not answered entity: Proposal help: What the client has been asked to decide and has not. Shown with the expiry date, because a proposal that lapses has to be rebuilt at current prices rather than simply re-sent. sql: SELECT p.proposal_number, y.display_name AS client, p.status, p.proposal_date, p.expiry_date, (p.expiry_date - now()::date) AS days_to_expiry FROM bus_proposal p JOIN bus_client c ON c.id = p.client_id LEFT JOIN bus_party y ON y.id = c.party_id WHERE p.deleted_at IS NULL AND p.status = 'presented' AND p.expiry_date >= now()::date ORDER BY p.expiry_date
    %%report client-proposals-expired title: Proposals that lapsed unanswered entity: Proposal help: Every one is work already done and thrown away. Read weekly it is a list of calls to make; read never, it is the reason the pipeline number is wrong. sql: SELECT p.proposal_number, y.display_name AS client, p.proposal_date, p.expiry_date FROM bus_proposal p JOIN bus_client c ON c.id = p.client_id LEFT JOIN bus_party y ON y.id = c.party_id WHERE p.deleted_at IS NULL AND (p.status = 'expired' OR (p.status = 'presented' AND p.expiry_date < now()::date)) ORDER BY p.expiry_date DESC
    %%report client-portfolios-by-objective title: Portfolios by stated objective entity: Portfolio chart: pie x: objective y: portfolios help: What the client asked the money to do, in their own words rather than the risk model's. Where this and the risk profile disagree, the suitability file is the thing that has to explain it. sql: SELECT COALESCE(NULLIF(p.investment_objective, ''), '(not stated)') AS objective, COUNT(*) AS portfolios FROM bus_portfolio p WHERE p.deleted_at IS NULL AND p.status = 'active' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY portfolios DESC
    %%report client-kyc-expiring title: Identity checks that expire within sixty days entity: KYCRecord help: A client whose verification lapses stops being someone the firm can trade for. Sixty days is enough to ask for a renewed document without the request sounding like an accusation. sql: SELECT c.client_number, y.display_name AS client, k.kyc_type, k.kyc_status, k.expiry_date, (k.expiry_date - current_date) AS days_left FROM bus_kyc_record k JOIN bus_client c ON c.id = k.client_id LEFT JOIN bus_party y ON y.id = c.party_id WHERE k.deleted_at IS NULL AND c.status = 'active' AND k.expiry_date BETWEEN current_date AND current_date + 60 ORDER BY k.expiry_date
    %%report client-signatures-outstanding title: Documents sent for signature and not signed entity: ESignatureRequest help: What the client has been asked to sign and has not. Anything older than a week has usually slipped behind an inbox, and the instruction it carries is not yet in force. sql: SELECT c.client_number, y.display_name AS client, e.status, e.requested_at, (current_date - e.requested_at::date) AS days_waiting FROM bus_e_signature_request e JOIN bus_client c ON c.id = e.client_id LEFT JOIN bus_party y ON y.id = c.party_id WHERE e.deleted_at IS NULL AND e.status IN ('requested', 'sent') AND e.requested_at < now() - interval '7 days' ORDER BY e.requested_at
    %%report client-documents-awaiting-review title: Documents received and not yet reviewed entity: Document help: A document a client has handed over that nobody has looked at. The client believes it is done; the file says it has not started. sql: SELECT d.file_name, d.status, d.uploaded_at, (current_date - d.uploaded_at::date) AS days_waiting FROM bus_document d WHERE d.deleted_at IS NULL AND d.status IN ('received', 'in_review') AND d.uploaded_at < now() - interval '14 days' ORDER BY d.uploaded_at
    %%report client-goals-by-status title: Goals by status entity: Goal chart: pie x: status y: goals help: How much of what clients said they wanted is identified, planned, funded or achieved. Read by a client it is progress; read by the firm it is the conversion rate of planning into money. sql: SELECT g.status, COUNT(*) AS goals, SUM(g.target_amount) AS target_amount FROM bus_goal g WHERE g.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY g.status ORDER BY goals DESC

%% -- dealer: the desk and what it trades ---------------------------------------
    %%report dealer-portfolios-without-risk-profile title: Portfolios with no risk profile attached entity: Portfolio help: A portfolio nobody can check a trade against. The dealing desk cannot assess suitability without it, so the trade either waits or goes through unchecked — and the second is the one that becomes a complaint. sql: SELECT p.portfolio_name, p.portfolio_type, p.base_currency, p.investment_objective, p.status FROM bus_portfolio p WHERE p.deleted_at IS NULL AND p.status = 'active' AND p.risk_profile_id IS NULL ORDER BY p.portfolio_name
    %%report dealer-portfolios-by-currency title: Live portfolios by base currency entity: Portfolio chart: bar x: currency y: portfolios help: What the desk has to be able to settle in. A currency with a handful of portfolios still needs the same custody arrangement as the main one, and that cost is invisible until somebody counts. sql: SELECT p.base_currency AS currency, p.portfolio_type, COUNT(*) AS portfolios FROM bus_portfolio p WHERE p.deleted_at IS NULL AND p.status = 'active' GROUP BY p.base_currency, p.portfolio_type ORDER BY portfolios DESC
    %%report dealer-orders-pipeline title: Orders in flight, by status entity: Order chart: bar x: status y: orders help: The dealing desk at a glance: what is waiting for approval, what has gone out and what is part-filled. A bar that grows from one day to the next is an order type the desk cannot clear. sql: SELECT o.status, COUNT(*) AS orders, SUM(o.amount) AS amount FROM bus_order o WHERE o.deleted_at IS NULL AND o.status IN ('pending_approval', 'approved', 'submitted', 'partially_executed') GROUP BY o.status ORDER BY orders DESC
    %%report dealer-orders-stuck title: Orders that have not moved in two days entity: Order help: An order that has sat in one state for two days is either waiting on somebody or lost between systems. Either way the client believes it is working. sql: SELECT o.order_type, o.status, p.portfolio_name, s.symbol, o.quantity, o.amount, o.ordered_at, (current_date - o.ordered_at::date) AS days_open FROM bus_order o LEFT JOIN bus_portfolio p ON p.id = o.portfolio_id LEFT JOIN bus_security s ON s.id = o.security_id WHERE o.deleted_at IS NULL AND o.status IN ('pending_approval', 'approved', 'submitted', 'partially_executed') AND o.ordered_at < now() - interval '2 days' ORDER BY o.ordered_at
    %%report dealer-transactions-failed title: Transactions that failed or were reversed entity: InvestmentTransaction help: Settlement exceptions in the last ninety days. Each one is a position the books and the custodian disagree about until it is chased, and the oldest are the ones that become write-offs. sql: SELECT t.transaction_date, t.transaction_type, t.status, p.portfolio_name, t.quantity, t.amount, t.price FROM bus_investment_transaction t LEFT JOIN bus_portfolio p ON p.id = t.portfolio_id WHERE t.deleted_at IS NULL AND t.status IN ('failed', 'reversed') AND t.transaction_date >= current_date - 90 ORDER BY t.transaction_date DESC
    %%report dealer-corporate-actions-pending title: Corporate actions announced and not applied entity: CorporateAction help: Splits, dividends and mergers change what the client owns whether or not the system has caught up. An action past its effective date and still pending means holdings are wrong today. sql: SELECT s.symbol, s.name AS security, a.action_type, a.status, a.record_date, a.effective_date, (current_date - a.effective_date) AS days_past FROM bus_corporate_action a LEFT JOIN bus_security s ON s.id = a.security_id WHERE a.deleted_at IS NULL AND a.status IN ('announced', 'pending') ORDER BY a.effective_date
    %%report dealer-top-holdings title: Largest holdings across all portfolios entity: Holding chart: bar x: security y: market_value help: Where the money is actually sitting, by security. A name that appears here far above the rest is a concentration the firm carries on behalf of every client who owns it at once. sql: SELECT COALESCE(s.symbol, '(unknown)') AS security, COUNT(DISTINCT h.portfolio_id) AS portfolios, SUM(h.market_value) AS market_value FROM bus_holding h LEFT JOIN bus_security s ON s.id = h.security_id WHERE h.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY s.symbol ORDER BY market_value DESC NULLS LAST LIMIT 15
    %%report dealer-sip-due title: Standing instructions due or overdue entity: SIPInstruction help: Recurring investments that should have run by now. The client has been told it happens every month; a missed run is the complaint that arrives with a statement. sql: SELECT c.client_number, y.display_name AS client, s.amount, s.frequency, s.next_execution_date, (current_date - s.next_execution_date) AS days_late FROM bus_sip_instruction s JOIN bus_client c ON c.id = s.client_id LEFT JOIN bus_party y ON y.id = c.party_id WHERE s.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.status = 'active' AND s.next_execution_date <= current_date + 7 ORDER BY s.next_execution_date

%% -- compliance: what a regulator looks for first ------------------------------
    %%report compliance-assessments-overdue title: Clients with no current suitability assessment entity: SuitabilityAssessment help: The single finding a regulator looks for. An active client whose most recent assessment is over a year old is being advised on a view of their circumstances that is out of date, and nothing prompts anyone. sql: SELECT c.client_number, y.display_name AS client, MAX(s.assessment_date) AS last_assessed, (now()::date - MAX(s.assessment_date)) AS days_since FROM bus_client c LEFT JOIN bus_party y ON y.id = c.party_id LEFT JOIN bus_suitability_assessment s ON s.client_id = c.id AND s.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.status = 'approved' WHERE c.deleted_at IS NULL AND c.status = 'active' GROUP BY c.id, c.client_number, y.display_name HAVING MAX(s.assessment_date) IS NULL OR MAX(s.assessment_date) < now()::date - 365 ORDER BY last_assessed NULLS FIRST
    %%report compliance-assessments-unapproved title: Assessments completed but never approved entity: SuitabilityAssessment help: The assessment was done and then stalled before sign-off, so the client counts as unassessed however much work went in. It is the cheapest compliance gap in the system to close. sql: SELECT c.client_number, y.display_name AS client, s.assessment_type, s.status, s.assessment_date, s.result FROM bus_suitability_assessment s JOIN bus_client c ON c.id = s.client_id LEFT JOIN bus_party y ON y.id = c.party_id WHERE s.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.status IN ('draft', 'pending') AND s.assessment_date < now()::date - 14 ORDER BY s.assessment_date
    %%report compliance-kyc-not-verified title: Active clients without a verified identity check entity: KYCRecord help: The single most serious finding in this list. An active client whose latest check is pending, rejected or expired is being served with an identity the firm cannot currently stand behind. sql: SELECT c.client_number, y.display_name AS client, k.kyc_type, k.kyc_status, k.verification_date, k.expiry_date FROM bus_kyc_record k JOIN bus_client c ON c.id = k.client_id LEFT JOIN bus_party y ON y.id = c.party_id WHERE k.deleted_at IS NULL AND c.deleted_at IS NULL AND c.status = 'active' AND k.kyc_status IN ('pending', 'rejected', 'expired') ORDER BY k.expiry_date NULLS FIRST
    %%report compliance-risk-profiles-expired title: Active clients whose risk profile has expired entity: RiskProfile help: Advice is given against a stated appetite for risk. A profile past its expiry date is a statement the client made about a past self, and every recommendation since leans on it. sql: SELECT c.client_number, y.display_name AS client, r.risk_category, r.status, r.effective_date, r.expiry_date, (current_date - r.expiry_date) AS days_expired FROM bus_risk_profile r JOIN bus_client c ON c.id = r.client_id LEFT JOIN bus_party y ON y.id = c.party_id WHERE r.deleted_at IS NULL AND c.status = 'active' AND (r.status = 'expired' OR r.expiry_date < current_date) ORDER BY r.expiry_date
    %%report compliance-recommendations-failed-suitability title: Recommendations that failed or need a suitability review entity: InvestmentRecommendation help: Advice the suitability check did not pass, still open. The ones marked for review are waiting on a human; the ones marked failed should be rejected rather than left to age into an accidental approval. sql: SELECT r.recommendation_date, r.recommendation_type, r.status, r.suitability_status, (current_date - r.recommendation_date) AS days_open FROM bus_investment_recommendation r WHERE r.deleted_at IS NULL AND r.suitability_status IN ('failed', 'requires_review') AND r.status NOT IN ('rejected', 'expired', 'executed') ORDER BY r.recommendation_date
    %%report compliance-licences-expiring title: Advisor licences expiring within ninety days entity: Advisor help: An adviser without a current licence cannot give regulated advice, whatever their client list says. Ninety days is the time a renewal needs, not the time it is allowed to take. sql: SELECT a.employee_number, y.display_name AS advisor, a.advisor_type, a.license_number, a.license_expiry, (a.license_expiry - current_date) AS days_left FROM bus_advisor a LEFT JOIN bus_party y ON y.id = a.party_id WHERE a.deleted_at IS NULL AND a.status = 'active' AND a.license_expiry <= current_date + 90 ORDER BY a.license_expiry
    %%report compliance-rebalances-unresolved title: Rebalances detected and not carried out entity: RebalanceRecommendation help: A portfolio that has drifted from its model and stayed there is a portfolio the client is not holding what they were sold. Failed attempts are listed with the unattempted, because to the client they are the same thing. sql: SELECT p.portfolio_name, r.status, r.deviation_amount, r.detected_date, r.reason, (current_date - r.detected_date) AS days_open FROM bus_rebalance_recommendation r JOIN bus_portfolio p ON p.id = r.portfolio_id WHERE r.deleted_at IS NULL AND (r.status = 'failed' OR (r.status IN ('detected', 'analysed', 'proposed') AND r.detected_date < current_date - 14)) ORDER BY r.detected_date
    %%report compliance-approvals-rejected title: Recommendation approvals that were rejected entity: RecommendationApproval help: What the second pair of eyes refused, in the order it happened. A pattern of refusals against one adviser is a training conversation; a pattern against one product is a product conversation. sql: SELECT a.approved_at, a.status, a.comments, r.recommendation_type, r.recommendation_date FROM bus_recommendation_approval a JOIN bus_investment_recommendation r ON r.id = a.investment_recommendation_id WHERE a.deleted_at IS NULL AND a.status IN ('rejected', 'revoked') ORDER BY a.approved_at DESC NULLS LAST LIMIT 100
    %%report compliance-overrides title: Records changed outside the normal path entity: AuditEvent help: The audit trail read the way an investigator reads it: not everything that happened, but the actions that overrode, cancelled or superseded something. These are the entries that need a reason recorded against them. sql: SELECT a."timestamp" AS occurred_at, a.user_reference, a.action, a.entity_type, a.entity_reference, a.source FROM bus_audit_event a WHERE a.deleted_at IS NULL AND a.action ILIKE ANY (ARRAY['%override%', '%cancel%', '%supersede%', '%delete%', '%reject%']) AND a."timestamp" > now() - interval '90 days' ORDER BY a."timestamp" DESC
    %%report compliance-activity-by-user title: Audited actions by user entity: AuditEvent chart: bar x: user_ref y: actions help: Not a productivity measure and must not be read as one. It is the denominator: a user with far more audited changes than the rest is either carrying more, or working around something. sql: SELECT a.user_reference AS user_ref, COUNT(*) AS actions, COUNT(DISTINCT a.entity_type) AS entity_types FROM bus_audit_event a WHERE a.deleted_at IS NULL AND a."timestamp" > now() - interval '30 days' GROUP BY a.user_reference ORDER BY actions DESC LIMIT 25

%% -- finance: the revenue the business is run on -------------------------------
    %%report finance-commission-outstanding title: Commission earned and not settled entity: Commission chart: bar x: commission_type y: amount help: What is owed to the firm, by the kind of business that generated it. Anything still unsettled after a quarter is a reconciliation nobody finished, not a payment that is late. sql: SELECT COALESCE(NULLIF(m.commission_type, ''), '(not set)') AS commission_type, COUNT(*) AS entries, SUM(m.amount) AS amount FROM bus_commission m WHERE m.deleted_at IS NULL AND m.status IN ('pending', 'active') GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY amount DESC
    %%report finance-commission-by-month title: Commission earned by month entity: Commission chart: line x: month y: amount help: The revenue line the business is run on. Earned date rather than received, because that is the month the work belongs to however long the settlement takes. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', m.earned_date), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, SUM(m.amount) AS amount, COUNT(*) AS entries FROM bus_commission m WHERE m.deleted_at IS NULL AND m.earned_date > now()::date - 365 GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report finance-commission-failures title: Commission entries in exception entity: Commission help: Entries the reconciliation could not match. Each is money the firm believes it earned and the counterparty does not, and every one has to be chased individually before it ages out. sql: SELECT c.client_number, y.display_name AS client, m.commission_type, m.amount, m.earned_date, m.status FROM bus_commission m LEFT JOIN bus_client c ON c.id = m.client_id LEFT JOIN bus_party y ON y.id = c.party_id WHERE m.deleted_at IS NULL AND m.status IN ('failed', 'rejected', 'cancelled') ORDER BY m.earned_date DESC
    %%report finance-aum-by-portfolio-type title: Assets under management by portfolio type entity: PortfolioValuation chart: bar x: portfolio_type y: net_value help: Net value at each portfolio's latest valuation, grouped by the kind of portfolio. It is the number the fee income is a percentage of, so a type that shrinks here shrinks in the revenue line a quarter later. sql: SELECT p.portfolio_type, COUNT(*) AS portfolios, SUM(v.net_value) AS net_value FROM (SELECT DISTINCT ON (portfolio_id) * FROM bus_portfolio_valuation WHERE deleted_at IS NULL ORDER BY portfolio_id, valuation_date DESC) v JOIN bus_portfolio p ON p.id = v.portfolio_id WHERE p.deleted_at IS NULL AND p.status = 'active' GROUP BY p.portfolio_type ORDER BY net_value DESC NULLS LAST
    %%report finance-aum-by-month title: Valued assets by month entity: PortfolioValuation chart: line x: month y: net_value help: The total of every valuation taken in the month. It is a trend, not a balance: months with fewer valuations read lower, which is why the count sits beside the sum. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', v.valuation_date), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS valuations, SUM(v.net_value) AS net_value FROM bus_portfolio_valuation v WHERE v.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report finance-fee-schedules-by-type title: Active fee schedules by type entity: FeeSchedule chart: pie x: fee_type y: schedules help: The price list the firm is actually running. Several schedules of one type is either a deliberate tier or a copy that nobody retired, and only the rates side by side say which. sql: SELECT f.fee_type, COUNT(*) AS schedules, MIN(f.rate) AS lowest_rate, MAX(f.rate) AS highest_rate FROM bus_fee_schedule f WHERE f.deleted_at IS NULL AND f.status = 'active' GROUP BY f.fee_type ORDER BY schedules DESC
    %%report finance-realised-gains-by-year title: Realised gains by tax year entity: CapitalGain chart: bar x: tax_year y: gain help: Gains and losses the clients will be taxed on, by year and by holding period. Short and long are kept apart because they are taxed differently and netting them hides the larger bill. sql: SELECT g.tax_year, g.gain_type, COUNT(*) AS disposals, SUM(g.gain_amount) AS gain FROM bus_capital_gain g WHERE g.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY g.tax_year, g.gain_type ORDER BY g.tax_year DESC, g.gain_type
    %%report finance-liabilities-in-default title: Client liabilities in default entity: Liability help: Debt a client has stopped paying. It changes what they can afford to invest, and it is something the plan assumed they could. sql: SELECT l.liability_type, l.status, l.outstanding_amount, l.interest_rate, l.maturity_date, l.monthly_payment FROM bus_liability l WHERE l.deleted_at IS NULL AND l.status = 'defaulted' ORDER BY l.outstanding_amount DESC NULLS LAST

%% -- administrator: whether the system is telling the truth --------------------
    %%report admin-audit-gaps title: Days with no audit activity at all entity: AuditEvent help: A silent day in an audit trail is either a genuinely quiet day or a collector that stopped writing. The difference matters enormously and only shows up when the days are counted rather than the events. sql: SELECT to_char(d.day, 'YYYY-MM-DD') AS day, COUNT(a.id) AS events FROM generate_series((now() - interval '30 days')::date, now()::date, interval '1 day') AS d(day) LEFT JOIN bus_audit_event a ON a."timestamp" >= d.day AND a."timestamp" < d.day + interval '1 day' AND a.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY d.day HAVING COUNT(a.id) = 0 ORDER BY d.day
    %%report admin-audit-by-source title: Where audited changes come from entity: AuditEvent chart: pie x: source y: events help: The mix of interactive, batch and integration traffic. A source that suddenly dominates is usually an integration retrying, and it will fill the audit store long before anybody notices the cost. sql: SELECT COALESCE(NULLIF(a.source, ''), '(not recorded)') AS source, COUNT(*) AS events FROM bus_audit_event a WHERE a.deleted_at IS NULL AND a."timestamp" > now() - interval '30 days' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY events DESC
    %%report admin-integrations-not-healthy title: Integrations that are not running entity: ExternalIntegration help: Every connection to the outside world with its last successful sync. One that has failed or paused is data the rest of the system is quietly working without. sql: SELECT i.integration_type, i.provider_name, i.status, i.last_sync_at, (current_date - i.last_sync_at::date) AS days_since_sync FROM bus_external_integration i WHERE i.deleted_at IS NULL AND i.status IN ('failed', 'paused') ORDER BY i.last_sync_at NULLS FIRST
    %%report admin-audit-changes-without-detail title: Changes recorded without what changed entity: AuditEvent help: An audit entry that says an update happened and not what it was is a log that cannot answer the question it exists for. Some are system events; the rest are a collector that stopped recording values. sql: SELECT a."timestamp" AS occurred_at, a.action, a.entity_type, a.entity_reference, a.source FROM bus_audit_event a WHERE a.deleted_at IS NULL AND a.before_value IS NULL AND a.after_value IS NULL AND a.action ILIKE '%update%' AND a."timestamp" > now() - interval '30 days' ORDER BY a."timestamp" DESC
    %%report admin-parties-without-contact title: Active parties with no way to reach them entity: Party help: A client record with no contact point is one the firm cannot warn about a lapsed document or a market event. It is the cheapest data-quality gap to close and the most embarrassing to discover during an incident. sql: SELECT p.display_name, p.status, p.party_type FROM bus_party p LEFT JOIN bus_contact_point cp ON cp.party_id = p.id AND cp.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE p.deleted_at IS NULL AND p.status = 'active' GROUP BY p.id, p.display_name, p.status, p.party_type HAVING COUNT(cp.id) = 0 ORDER BY p.display_name LIMIT 200
